# A placeholder is one word

Every `placeholder=` and `data-placeholder=` on a text box is **one lowercase word**: what goes in the box, named (`search`, `name`, `username`, `message`, `reason`, `filter`). No sentences, no "optional", no examples in prose, no character rules.

The description goes somewhere that stays when the box has text in it:

- a `<label>` (visible, or `sr-only`) or an `aria-label` names the box for a screen reader. A placeholder is not a label: it goes away on the first key.
- a hint line under the box (`Field`'s `hint`, or a small `text-xs text-textTertiary` `<p>` tied on with `aria-describedby`) carries a rule the typer needs while typing, like the allowed characters `a-z 0-9 _ -`.

A link box may show a bare example as its one token (`https://twitch.tv/you`, `SOCIAL_EXAMPLE` in `infra/socials.ts`).

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — the box's only name is a sentence that vanishes on the first key
<input id="friends-search" placeholder="find someone or a group" />
<input id="auth-name" placeholder="a-z 0-9 _ -" />

// ✅ GOOD — one word in the box, the description in the label, the rule under it
<label htmlFor="friends-search" className="sr-only">find someone or a group</label>
<input id="friends-search" placeholder="search" />

<input id="auth-name" placeholder="username" aria-describedby="auth-name-hint" />
<p id="auth-name-hint" className="text-xs text-textTertiary">a-z 0-9 _ -</p>
```
